# AWS Configuration
# Required for AWS Bedrock access

# AWS Region (default: us-east-1)
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=us-east-1

# AWS Credentials
# Get these from AWS IAM Console or AWS CLI
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=your_access_key_here
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=your_secret_key_here

# AWS Session Token (optional, only needed for temporary credentials)
# Leave empty if using permanent IAM user credentials
AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=your_session_token_here

# AWS Bedrock Knowledge Base ID
# Get this from AWS Bedrock Console > Knowledge Bases
# Format: 10 alphanumeric characters (e.g., ABCDEF1234)
KNOWLEDGE_BASE_ID=your_knowledge_base_id_here

# ============================================
# Setup Instructions
# ============================================

# 1. Copy this file to .env:
#    cp .env.example .env
#
# 2. Fill in your AWS credentials:
#    - AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: Your AWS access key
#    - AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: Your AWS secret key
#    - AWS_SESSION_TOKEN: (Optional) Only if using temporary credentials
#
# 3. Fill in your Knowledge Base ID:
#    - Go to AWS Bedrock Console
#    - Navigate to Knowledge Bases
#    - Copy your Knowledge Base ID
#
# 4. Save the file
#
# 5. Start the application:
#    start_servers.bat

# ============================================
# How to Get AWS Credentials
# ============================================

# Option 1: AWS IAM User (Permanent Credentials)
# 1. Go to AWS IAM Console
# 2. Create a new user or use existing user
# 3. Attach policy: AmazonBedrockFullAccess
# 4. Create access key
# 5. Copy Access Key ID and Secret Access Key
# 6. No session token needed

# Option 2: AWS SSO / Temporary Credentials
# 1. Run: aws sso login
# 2. Run: aws configure export-credentials --profile your-profile
# 3. Copy all three values (including session token)
# 4. Note: Session token expires (usually 1-12 hours)

# Option 3: AWS CLI
# 1. Run: aws configure
# 2. Enter your credentials
# 3. Credentials stored in ~/.aws/credentials
# 4. App will use these automatically

# ============================================
# How to Create Knowledge Base
# ============================================

# 1. Go to AWS Bedrock Console
# 2. Navigate to Knowledge Bases
# 3. Click "Create knowledge base"
# 4. Upload your documents (markdown files)
# 5. Configure embeddings model
# 6. Create and wait for indexing
# 7. Copy the Knowledge Base ID

# ============================================
# Local Fallback
# ============================================

# If you don't have AWS Bedrock access:
# - Set KNOWLEDGE_BASE_ID=ABCDEF1234 (dummy value)
# - App will use local knowledge base fallback
# - Files from: data_package/knowledge_base/*.md

# ============================================
# Security Notes
# ============================================

# - NEVER commit .env file to git
# - .env is in .gitignore by default
# - Rotate credentials regularly
# - Use IAM roles in production
# - Limit permissions to minimum required

# ============================================
# Troubleshooting
# ============================================

# Error: "Unable to locate credentials"
# Solution: Check AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID and AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY

# Error: "ExpiredToken"
# Solution: Refresh AWS_SESSION_TOKEN (if using temporary credentials)

# Error: "AccessDeniedException"
# Solution: Add AmazonBedrockFullAccess policy to IAM user

# Error: "ResourceNotFoundException" (Knowledge Base)
# Solution: Check KNOWLEDGE_BASE_ID is correct

# ============================================
# Testing
# ============================================

# Test AWS credentials:
# aws bedrock list-foundation-models --region us-east-1

# Test Knowledge Base:
# aws bedrock-agent-runtime retrieve \
#   --knowledge-base-id YOUR_KB_ID \
#   --retrieval-query text="test" \
#   --region us-east-1
